t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5{c}_{1}=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2\\cdot 1+{c}_{2}=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129313 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "833d0ec0-d895-5d66-b2ad-d058858caf9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8b0460a-d0ad-5d76-b63d-e1be79a2bf53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\frac{9}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390779} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a14dfc-50da-56c0-9bcf-ac663a34125d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-\\frac{7}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390780} {:answer/id #uuid "1dbcec44-0f8c-57c6-8f12-e880f0d06cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-\\frac{9}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390781} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1d5dc2-3635-56a3-a363-0ad6d716256c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}-\\frac{7}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390782} {:answer/id #uuid "a1dd86f6-75a2-50a1-af44-6ffa5adbb75f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{7}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b1d5dc2-3635-56a3-a363-0ad6d716256c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}-\\frac{7}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390782} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb599c70-6dbd-5227-961a-371c124bc72d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45696d2e-86e2-52d9-b755-e4c9e51775a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-326437" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}=1 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting into the second equation, we obtain ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{7}{2}.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}-\\frac{7}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129313 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "833d0ec0-d895-5d66-b2ad-d058858caf9e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9bda6e-7e9f-5f46-a9ff-034b8fce5b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=37[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}-14[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370067} {:answer/id #uuid "45848a5f-be44-5690-ada7-4e4b5a8d8ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=-6[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}-9[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370068} {:answer/id #uuid "e2268519-5a54-5d15-9fee-de95bcecd9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+6[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370069} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f6c8dc-a0b0-5c19-9a91-27f5d9dd3149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=16[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+7[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370070} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a257d5-e7ac-5472-bc75-90a08ae3e50b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=6[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}-7[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b9bda6e-7e9f-5f46-a9ff-034b8fce5b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=37[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}-14[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370067} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ea9e031-e55c-53ff-bb1f-586d3945e9f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f87900f-7ab3-5049-8d96-2a75cd0b00fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-327153" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3/2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2}=14\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{2}=-14$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-2(-14)=9\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=37.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =37[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}-14[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326756 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35a8694d-4fa1-54a6-a492-3025cb627fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1def90c1-295c-513f-8d17-08d3eb728d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{28}+18{e}^{-7} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324223} {:answer/id #uuid "9c44ae5b-89ec-5509-b374-71c79cd546e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18{e}^{28}+24{e}^{-7} \\\\ -6{e}^{28}+18{e}^{-7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325296} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f9a8f0-7ca4-532a-8c5c-a15bc32f000b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-18{e}^{7}+24{e}^{-7} \\\\ -18{e}^{7}+18{e}^{-7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325689} {:answer/id #uuid "d23922bc-ad96-548d-bd19-9c0401a8f083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-18{e}^{28}+24{e}^{-7} \\\\ -18{e}^{28}+18{e}^{-7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325797} {:answer/id #uuid "e07bab0e-82d0-59c4-900c-aee316593406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12{e}^{28}+28{e}^{-7} \\\\ -18{e}^{28}+16{e}^{-7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d23922bc-ad96-548d-bd19-9c0401a8f083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-18{e}^{28}+24{e}^{-7} \\\\ -18{e}^{28}+18{e}^{-7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325797} :answer-field/id #uuid "92bb4f1b-aee1-5145-b7ea-470f04edbde6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2ab3368-1586-5095-a52c-feadd0cc4835" :question/math-academy-id "q-39280" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $x(t),$ and that $x(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nfind the position of the particle at $t=7$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2} \\\\ -{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}=6 \\\\ -{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $-1$ and adding the result to the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-{c}_{1}+4\\cdot 6=6\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=18.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =18\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $x(7)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(7) & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4\\cdot 7}+[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-7} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{28}+[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-7} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18{e}^{28}+24{e}^{-7} \\\\ -18{e}^{28}+18{e}^{-7}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
